Product Marketplace API
Overview
This project is a backend application for managing products in a business context. Built with Django and Django REST Framework (DRF), it provides a RESTful API for businesses to create, update, and manage products with an approval workflow before products become publicly visible.

This backend serves as the foundation for a product management system and is designed to integrate seamlessly with frontend applications, such as the Product Marketplace Dashboard UI mockup.

Key Features
CRUD Operations – Full create, read, update, and delete functionality for products
Approval Workflow – Three-stage status progression: draft → pending → approved
Business Association – Products are linked to specific businesses
User Ownership – Track product creators with user-based ownership
Timestamped Records – Automatic creation timestamps for audit trails
Role-Based Permissions – Secure access control for different user types
Public API Endpoint – Separate read-only access for approved products

Technologies Used
Python 3.11+ – Core programming language
Django 4.x – High-level web framework for rapid development
Django REST Framework (DRF) – Powerful toolkit for building Web APIs
SQLite – Lightweight database for development (easily swappable for production)
Git – Version control and collaboration


Getting Started
Prerequisites
Python 3.11 or higher installed
pip (Python package manager)
Git
A code editor (VS Code, PyCharm, etc.)
1. Clone the Repository
git clone https://github.com/baisoft-product-marketplace/backend.git
cd backend

2. Create a Virtual Environment
# Create virtual environment
python -m venv venv

# Activate virtual environment
# On Linux/macOS:
source venv/bin/activate

# On Windows:
venv\Scripts\activate

# You should see (venv) in your terminal prompt

3. Install Dependencies
pip install -r requirements.txt

Expected dependencies:
Django
djangorestframework
django-cors-headers (for frontend integration)
django-filter (optional, for advanced filtering)
python-decouple (optional, for environment variables)

4. Apply Database Migrations
# Create database tables
python manage.py migrate

# You should see output confirming migrations were applied

5. Create a Superuser (Admin Account)
python manage.py createsuperuser

# Follow the prompts to create an admin account
# Username: admin
# Email: admin@example.com
# Password: (choose a secure password)

6. Run the Development Server
python manage.py runserver

# Server will start at http://127.0.0.1:8000/

Role Assignment

Business Admins assign roles to users.

New users registering via the API (POST /api/users/register/) default to viewer role.

Admin users can promote viewer users to editor or approver via Django shell.

Assigning Roles via Django Shell
python manage.py shell


1. Create a business

from users.models import Business
biz = Business.objects.create(name="TechCorp")


2. Create a new user as a viewer

from users.models import User
new_user = User.objects.create_user(username="jane", password="pass123", business=biz)
print(new_user.role)  # Output: 'viewer'


3. Admin assigns a new role

user = User.objects.get(username="jane")
user.role = "editor"  # or "approver" or "admin"
user.save()


4. Verify users

User.objects.all()


Assumptions & Limitations:

Role assignment is currently done via shell, not yet implemented in API endpoints for admins.

Each user must belong to a business.

Superuser is separate from business-level roles.

# Testing

Run all tests:

python manage.py test


Testing Details:

Tests cover CRUD operations, approval workflow, and role-based access control

Example test cases:

Editors can create products but cannot approve

Approvers can approve products

Users can only see products belonging to their business

Some tests may fail if roles or business associations are not correctly created

# Implementation Details

Product Model

Fields: name, description, price, status, business, created_by, created_at

Status workflow: draft → pending → approved

ProductViewSet

Handles authenticated CRUD operations for products

Only owners can modify their products

Uses DRF ModelViewSet with proper permissions

PublicProductViewSet

Read-only view for listing approved products

Accessible to all users without authentication

# Routing

Uses DefaultRouter to register /products/ and /public-products/ endpoints

# Tech Decisions, Assumptions, and Limitations

Decision: Role-based permissions are implemented at the DRF ViewSet level.

Decision: Products are linked to businesses and users to enforce ownership.

Assumption: Admin of a business manually assigns roles via shell for now.

Limitation: Role assignment API endpoints were not built due to time constraints and a power outage during refinement.

Limitation: Only basic filtering (min_price, max_price, status) is implemented.

